  "Question": "When simplified and expressed with negative exponents, the expression \\((x+y)^{-1}(x^{-1}+y^{-1})\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x^{-2}+2x^{-1}y^{-1}+y^{-2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(x^{-2}+2^{-1}x^{-1}y^{-1}+y^{-2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(x^{-1}y^{-1}\\)",
    "D": "\\(x^{-2}+y^{-2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{x^{-1}y^{-1}}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A negative first power means a reciprocal. The original expression therefore requires \\(x\\ne0\\), \\(y\\ne0\\), and \\(x+y\\ne0\\). Combining the two reciprocals reveals a factor that can cancel:\n\\[\nx^{-1}+y^{-1}\n=\\frac1x+\\frac1y\n=\\frac{x+y}{xy}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n(x+y)^{-1}(x^{-1}+y^{-1})\n=\\frac1{x+y}\\cdot\\frac{x+y}{xy}\n=\\frac1{xy}\n=x^{-1}y^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid because \\(x+y\\ne0\\). With all three original restrictions retained, the simplified expression is \\(x^{-1}y^{-1}\\), choice C.",
